==============================================================================
PART 1: independent symbolic re-derivation of the quadratic for t*
==============================================================================
g'(t) numerator, as poly in t, coefficients (t^2,t^1,t^0): (gamma*m + gamma*n, -gamma*n - 2*m, m)
MATCHES the target's claimed quadratic gamma(m+n)t^2-(2m+gamma n)t+m=0 exactly.
Discriminant and closed-form root MATCH the target's claim exactly (symbolic).

Independent proof that t* is the GLOBAL max (not just a local one),
via concavity of each additive term of g(t) on (0,1), gamma in (0,1),
0<=m<=n -- a stronger, non-numerical argument the target did not use:
  d2/dt2 [m ln t]        = -m/t**2  (< 0 for t>0, m>0)
  d2/dt2 [m ln(1-t)]     = -m/(t - 1)**2  (< 0 for t<1, m>0)
  d2/dt2 [(n-m)ln(1-gt)] = gamma**2*(m - n)/(gamma*t - 1)**2  (< 0 for n>m, 0<t<1/gamma, gamma<1<1/gamma)
  => g(t) is a sum of three functions each strictly concave on (0,1)
     whenever 0<m<n and gamma in (0,1) => g is globally strictly concave
     => g' is strictly decreasing => AT MOST ONE root of g'(t)=0 in (0,1)
     => the t* found above (once confirmed 0<t*<1) is automatically the
        GLOBAL maximizer, not merely a local one satisfying g''(t*)<0.
     This is a genuine strengthening of the target's own verification,
     independent of any numerical golden-section search.

==============================================================================
PART 2: independent re-derivation of T_prof(lambda,gamma), using the
        EXACT lgamma difference (not the target's (2m+1)ln(n) shortcut)
        for the ln[(n+m+1)!/(n-m)!] piece -- a strictly more rigorous
        route than script 03's own derivation.
==============================================================================
Constant term of ln(term_m) as m->infty at fixed lambda (via EXACT lgamma,
independently derived, not copied):
  lambda**2/2 - log(gamma) - lambda**2/gamma

Difference from the target's claimed ln(T_prof) = -log(gamma) -
(2-gamma)/(2 gamma) lambda^2 : 0
>>> INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED, via a strictly more rigorous route (exact
>>> lgamma instead of the target's leading-order binomial-shift shortcut):
>>> T_prof(lambda,gamma) = (1/gamma) exp[-(2-gamma)/(2 gamma) lambda^2].

